INT. CANAL LOCK-KEEPER'S COTTAGE - NIGHT

1841. A single room: iron stove, rough table, a rain-black window. The canal sloshes against the lock gates outside.

AGNES THORNBURY, 40s, widow, plain brown dress, sits at the table peeling a potato. A hard knock at the door. She sets down the knife, crosses the room, and opens it a crack.

A MAN, 30s, drenched, hatless, bleeding from one temple, stands in the rain. He holds a canvas satchel against his chest like a child.

THE STRANGER
Forgive me. My boat has sunk below your lock.

AGNES
Then you'll want the bank, not my door.

THE STRANGER
I'll want a fire, if you've one to spare.

She considers him. She steps back. He enters, dripping, and kneels at the stove. She locks the door behind him and slides the bolt. He notices. He says nothing.

AGNES
Your head.

THE STRANGER
A gate struck me. It's nothing.

AGNES
It is something. Sit.

He sits. She takes a rag from a peg, wets it from the kettle, and presses it against his temple. He flinches, then goes still. His eyes fix on the satchel in his lap. Hers do the same.

THE STRANGER
You're kind.

AGNES
I'm careful. There's a difference.

THE STRANGER
Which are you being now?

AGNES
Both.

He laughs, once, and winces. She steps back, folds the rag, and crosses to the shelf. From behind a tin of tea she takes a long hunting knife and lays it on the table between them, blade toward him.

AGNES (CONT'D)
My husband kept that for poachers.

THE STRANGER
Then I am glad not to be one.

AGNES
What's in the satchel?

THE STRANGER
Letters.

AGNES
Whose?

THE STRANGER
My brother's. He drowned in this canal in March. I came to see where.

AGNES
The Haverty boy?

THE STRANGER
You knew him.

AGNES
I pulled him out. I buried him myself.

The Stranger rises so fast the chair falls over. Agnes takes up the knife in one smooth motion. They face each other across the table, breathing hard.

THE STRANGER
Where?

AGNES
Behind the lock, under the willow.

THE STRANGER
Take me there.

AGNES
In this weather?

THE STRANGER
Now.

She lowers the knife. Her shoulders drop. She takes a lantern from the wall and lights it with a taper from the stove. The Stranger picks up the fallen chair, sets it right, and stands waiting, hat in hand though he has no hat.

AGNES (CONT'D)
What do I call you?

JULIAN
Julian Haverty.

AGNES
Julian. I am Agnes.

JULIAN
I know. He wrote of you.

AGNES
He wrote of me?

JULIAN
Every letter. He loved you.

Agnes sets down the lantern. Her hand covers her mouth. For the first time she looks at the satchel not with suspicion but with grief.

AGNES
He never said.

JULIAN
He meant to.

She nods. She picks the lantern up again. They go out together into the rain. The door swings in the wind.
